INSERT INTO interview_questions (seed_key,topic,difficulty,prompt,model_answer,rubric) VALUES
('dsa-hashmap','DSA','INTERMEDIATE','How does a hash map handle collisions, and what affects its performance?','A hash map maps a key hash to a bucket and resolves collisions using a structure such as a list or tree. Good hash distribution and a controlled load factor keep lookup near constant time on average; pathological collisions can make it slower.','Cover buckets, collision handling, load factor, and average-versus-worst-case behavior.'),
('dsa-complexity','DSA','BEGINNER','What is the time complexity of binary search, and what condition does it require?','Binary search takes O(log n) comparisons because it halves the candidate range each step. The input must be ordered, and the searched range must support efficient midpoint access for the usual array implementation.','Identify logarithmic complexity and sorted input.'),
('oop-solid','OOP','ADVANCED','Choose one SOLID principle and explain a design change that follows it.','For example, the open/closed principle suggests extending behavior through a stable abstraction rather than repeatedly editing a central conditional. A strategy interface can let new pricing rules be added without changing existing callers.','Explain a principle, concrete design, and its tradeoff.'),
('os-process-thread','OPERATING_SYSTEMS','BEGINNER','How does a process differ from a thread?','A process has its own virtual address space and operating-system resources. Threads execute within a process and share its memory while retaining their own stacks and scheduling state.','Mention process isolation and shared process memory among threads.'),
('os-deadlock','OPERATING_SYSTEMS','INTERMEDIATE','What conditions are necessary for deadlock, and how can a system reduce its risk?','Deadlock requires mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, and circular wait. Consistent lock ordering can prevent circular wait; timeouts or detection and recovery are other approaches.','Name all four conditions and one mitigation.'),
('net-http','NETWORKS','BEGINNER','What is the difference between TCP and UDP?','TCP provides a reliable ordered byte stream with connection management and retransmission. UDP sends datagrams without those guarantees and has lower protocol overhead, so applications choose based on their delivery needs.','Compare reliability/order with datagram-oriented delivery.'),
('net-tls','NETWORKS','ADVANCED','At a high level, what does TLS provide to an HTTPS connection?','TLS negotiates cryptographic parameters, authenticates the server using certificates, and establishes keys for encrypted and integrity-protected communication. Correct certificate validation is essential to prevent impersonation.','Mention confidentiality, integrity, authentication, and certificate validation.'),
('rest-idempotency','REST_APIS','INTERMEDIATE','What does idempotency mean for an HTTP operation, and why does it matter for retries?','An idempotent operation has the same intended effect when repeated. Safe retries after a timeout can avoid duplicate changes; a server may use an idempotency key for non-idempotent actions such as creating a submission.','Define repeated effect and connect it to safe retries.'),
('rest-status','REST_APIS','BEGINNER','When would an API return 201, 204, and 409?','201 indicates a resource was created, 204 indicates successful completion with no response body, and 409 indicates a conflict with the current resource state such as a duplicate request.','Give the meaning of each status code.'),
('system-cache','SYSTEM_DESIGN','INTERMEDIATE','What tradeoffs should a team consider before adding a cache?','A cache can reduce latency and load but introduces stale data, invalidation, memory cost, and another failure mode. Choose the data and consistency window, keying strategy, eviction policy, and fallback behavior deliberately.','Balance latency/load benefits against staleness, invalidation, and failures.'),
('system-scale','SYSTEM_DESIGN','ADVANCED','How would you approach a service that is becoming slow as traffic grows?','Measure first with request and database metrics, identify the bottleneck, and establish a target. Depending on evidence, optimize queries, add indexes or caching, scale stateless instances, and use queues for asynchronous work while preserving correctness.','Ask for measurement and bottleneck evidence before proposing changes.'),
('behavioral-conflict','BEHAVIORAL','INTERMEDIATE','Describe a disagreement with a teammate and how you worked through it.','A strong response gives a concrete context, listens to the other person, explains the evidence and options, agrees on a decision or experiment, and reflects on the result.','Look for respectful listening, concrete actions, outcome, and reflection.'),
('java-collections','JAVA','BEGINNER','When would you choose a Set rather than a List?','A Set is useful when uniqueness and membership checks matter. A List preserves order and allows duplicates. The right choice depends on required behavior and iteration/access patterns.','Compare uniqueness/membership with order/duplicates.'),
('sql-transactions','SQL','ADVANCED','Why can two concurrent transactions produce a lost update, and how can it be prevented?','Both transactions can read the same value and then overwrite each other. Optimistic version checks, row locks, or an isolation level that detects the conflict can prevent silent loss; retry behavior must be considered.','Explain the race and name a concurrency control option.');
